// std/process.jtr — running an external command, behind an explicit capability.
//
// TIER: `std`. Requires a shell. Thin named wrappers over the `run_command` and
// `eprint_str` intrinsics, in the `io.jtr` / `env.jtr` shape — when `extern "c"`
// eventually retires them, only this module changes (design §14).
//
// WHY A HANDLE AND NOT A FREE `run(cmd)`. Executing a command is the single most
// dangerous thing this standard library can do, and `run_command` is an ambient
// intrinsic: any function anywhere can reach it, and no signature reveals that it
// did. A `Process` value is the smallest fix — the ability to spawn becomes
// something a caller must be *given*, so `fn build(mut p: Process, ...)` announces
// in its type that it may execute commands and `fn parse(read src: str)` announces
// that it may not.
//
// This is not a sandbox. Nothing stops a function from calling `run_command`
// directly, and nothing here can. `@no_alloc` has the same shape of limitation and
// is still worth having: the value is that the honest path is also the documented
// one, and that a reviewer can see the capability in the signature. A real
// enforcement story needs the compiler to treat effects as part of a type, which
// is a language question and not a library one.
//
// `denied()` is the other half of the point. It is a handle that refuses to
// execute anything while still COUNTING what was asked of it, so a test can hand
// one to code under test and afterwards assert both that nothing ran and that the
// right number of attempts were made. `process_demo.jtr` proves the refusal is
// real rather than cosmetic: it runs a file-creating command through a `host()`
// handle and finds the file, then through a `denied()` handle and finds nothing.
//
// EXIT CODES ARE PORTABLE NOW, AND THEY DID NOT USED TO BE. The runtime helper was
// `return (int32_t)system(cp)` — the raw `system()` result. On Windows that is the
// command's exit code; POSIX specifies a *wait status* with the code in the high
// byte, so `exit 3` was 3 on Windows and 768 on Linux, and only zero coincided.
// This module worked around it by steering callers to `run_ok`.
//
// The helper now applies `WEXITSTATUS` on POSIX, so **`run` returns the exit code
// on every platform** and may be compared against a literal. `-1` is reserved for
// "did not exit normally" — signalled, or the shell could not be started — which is
// distinguishable from any real exit code, since those are 0..255.
//
// It was normalised in the INTRINSIC rather than here, because the library cannot
// see which platform it is on: the intrinsic list is the platform boundary until a
// `sys` tier exists. `run_ok` remains the right spelling when all you want is
// success, but it is no longer the *only* portable one.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/process_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/process_test.jtr

// The status `run` reports when a denied handle refuses. Deliberately NOT a
// reliable discriminator: a real `system()` failure can also be -1. Use
// `refused(p)` or `can_run(p)` to tell refusal from failure — the count is exact,
// the code is a courtesy. (Prefixed because a module `const` is emitted under its
// bare name, so two modules cannot share one; see std/test.jtr.)
const PROC_DENIED: i32 = 0 - 1

// The capability. Scalars only — a Jestyr borrow is second-class, so a handle
// cannot own borrowed storage; see `std/test.jtr` for the same constraint shaping
// the same kind of value.
pub struct Process {
    allowed: bool,
    runs: i64,
    refused: i64,
    last: i32,
}

// A handle that can actually execute. Naming it `host` rather than `new` is the
// point: acquiring the authority to run commands should read like an act.
pub fn host() -> Process {
    return Process{ allowed: true, runs: 0, refused: 0, last: 0 }
}

// A handle that executes nothing and counts every attempt. For tests, dry runs,
// and any subsystem that should be able to *describe* work without doing it.
pub fn denied() -> Process {
    return Process{ allowed: false, runs: 0, refused: 0, last: 0 }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the queries

// Will this handle execute? False for `denied()`.
@no_alloc pub fn can_run(read p: Process) -> bool {
    return p.allowed
}

// How many commands this handle actually executed.
@no_alloc pub fn runs(read p: Process) -> i64 {
    return p.runs
}

// How many commands this handle refused to execute. Always 0 for `host()`; for
// `denied()` this is the assertion a test wants — "it tried to invoke gcc twice".
@no_alloc pub fn refused(read p: Process) -> i64 {
    return p.refused
}

// The raw status of the most recent `run`/`run_ok` through this handle, or 0 if
// nothing has run. Platform-specific when non-zero — see the header.
@no_alloc pub fn last_status(read p: Process) -> i32 {
    return p.last
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the actions

// Execute `cmd` through the shell and return the RAW platform status, recording
// the attempt. A denied handle executes nothing and returns `PROC_DENIED`.
//
// Not `@no_alloc`: the runtime NUL-terminates the command string on the heap, and
// the command itself may do anything at all.
pub fn run(mut p: Process, read cmd: str) -> i32 {
    if p.allowed == false {
        p.refused = p.refused + 1
        p.last = PROC_DENIED
        return PROC_DENIED
    }
    let rc: i32 = run_command(cmd)
    p.runs = p.runs + 1
    p.last = rc
    return rc
}

// Execute `cmd` and report whether it SUCCEEDED. Prefer it when success is the only
// question — it says so at the call site, and it cannot be misread the way a bare
// `== 0` on an integer can. (It is no longer the only PORTABLE spelling: `run`'s
// exit code is normalised across platforms now.)
// A denied handle refuses, which is reported as failure.
pub fn run_ok(mut p: Process, read cmd: str) -> bool {
    return run(p, cmd) == 0
}

// Write one line to STDERR. The diagnostic channel, kept separate from stdout so
// that a program whose stdout is data — a compiler emitting C, a tool emitting
// JSON — can report progress without corrupting it. Takes no handle: writing a
// diagnostic is not the capability worth gating, and pretending otherwise would
// make the handle look more like a sandbox than it is.
pub fn note(read text: str) {
    eprint_str(text)
}
